# SOLID (Strict, Pragmatic)

Apply SOLID to **real code**, not as an excuse for extra layers. Full reference: `docs/ARCHITECTURE_CODING_STANDARDS.md`.

## Single Responsibility (S)

- One reason to change per function, class, component, or module.
- Bun: `routes` parse HTTP; `service` orchestrates; `domain` holds game rules; `repository` persists.
- React: components render; hooks fetch/state; `lib/api/` calls the backend.

## Open/Closed (O)

- Extend with new functions or composed types — not inheritance hierarchies or plugin registries.
- New behavior = new code path behind existing seam, not a framework.

## Liskov Substitution (L)

- Test fakes and production implementations must honor the same contract (errors, invariants, game-state consistency).
- Breaking a port's contract is a bug — fix the implementation, not the caller's expectations.

## Interface Segregation (I)

- Small interfaces at IO boundaries only (database repository, email provider, WebSocket transport).
- No "god interfaces" with unused methods.

## Dependency Inversion (D)

- Domain and service depend on abstractions; `repository`, `email`, and `websocket` implement them.
- Wire dependencies in `server.ts` / `app.ts` — no global singletons.
- **YAGNI gate:** no interface until a second implementation or test fake needs it.

## SOLID vs YAGNI

| SOLID says | YAGNI limits |
|---|---|
| Depend on abstractions at boundaries | Don't create abstractions before the boundary exists |
| One responsibility | Don't split one function into five files prematurely |
| Open for extension | Don't build extension points nothing extends yet |

When SOLID and YAGNI conflict, **YAGNI wins** until a second use case appears.
